## Why This Is Asked
Evaluates practical, beginner-appropriate streaming ingestion using common AWS data tooling and basic quality controls.

## Key Concepts
- Kinesis Data Streams for real-time data ingestion
- AWS Glue ETL and Data Catalog for data processing and metadata management
- Parquet format with date-based partitioning for optimized storage and queries
- Simple schema evolution strategies for handling changing data structures
- Data quality checks (required fields, types, nulls)
- CloudWatch alarms and end-to-end testing

## Code Example
```javascript
// Pseudo-code to start a Glue job
const glue = new AWS.Glue();
await glue.startJobRun({
  JobName: 'mobile-events-etl',
  Arguments: {
    '--input-stream': 'mobile-app-events',
    '--output-path': 's3://analytics-bucket/events/',
    '--partition-format': 'yyyy/MM/dd'
  }
});
```

## Why This Is Asked

Assesses practical, beginner-friendly data ingestion with Glue, including partitioned Parquet output, catalog updates, and basic quality checks.

## Key Concepts

- AWS Glue Spark jobs
- Parquet partitioning by date
- Glue Data Catalog
- Schema evolution (nullable columns)
- Data quality checks (not-null, timestamp validity)

## Code Example

```javascript
// Data quality check example (JS-like pseudocode for illustration)
function validRecord(rec){
  if (!rec.user_id || typeof rec.user_id !== 'string') return false;
  if (!rec.event_ts || isNaN(Date.parse(rec.event_ts))) return false;
  return true;
}
```

## Follow-up Questions

- How would you handle late-arriving files or reprocessing failed files without duplicating data?
- How would you adapt the pipeline for multi-tenant inputs with evolving schemas?
